41 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069776 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069776 #edb/ref 1061 40513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069776 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2bc45b7b-b768-5e67-92eb-6d8dfdb26080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069776 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069777 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069777 #edb/ref 1061 40520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069777 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "92bbfce1-1705-5e28-a0bf-e544d5fecdf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069777 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069778 #edb/ref 1061 40523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069778 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "57fbfe53-6167-5a8c-ac3b-d190aa02b687" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069778 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069779 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069779 #edb/ref 1061 40568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069779 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25606295-3ea3-509a-91f7-294ef43bb1c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069779 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069780 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069780 #edb/ref 1061 40586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069780 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d917615e-b6ee-5929-8832-7bcfe670df91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069780 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069883 #edb/ref 1061 5562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069883 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5832944b-422f-5154-8451-6dc2929473ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069883 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069884 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069884 #edb/ref 1061 5564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069884 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "423acfad-41bd-5f38-909c-92b01da8da23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069884 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069934 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to represent the following number line graph using interval notation:\n\n![](images/0b/0bf5e420e2929032c11964e15d710014fb34d559aa065428b9d57fff14d10eb0.png)\n\nThis number line consists of the interval $(-3, -1)$ as well as the interval $(1, 3).$\n\nTo express the combination of these two intervals, we create a **union of intervals** using the **union sign** $\\cup$ as follows: \n\n$$\n(-3, -1) \\cup (1, 3)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069934 #edb/ref 1065 1422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069934 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3a3780a2-b10d-5640-a797-ea56e4eee1af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069934 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069950 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, the union of two intervals can simplify into a single interval. For example, consider the union\n\n$$\n(-3,2) \\cup (0,4).\n$$\n\nIf we plot these two intervals on number lines, we get the following:\n\n![](images/4a/4aafe0cdf9f5b27d99538bbaacbf567ded823189399e518f2ead7381da35e84c.png)\n\n![](images/4a/4a26c3aa30f2aab862b31624ba08e3a575fee511e46f89abf62c26aa38dd811f.png)\n\nThe union of the two intervals consists of all the points which are contained within at least one of the intervals. So, to graph the union of the two intervals, we take the graphs of the individual intervals and lay them on top of each other.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/83/835f7e883cf1aba1945f9cecab382ddc163e1c2413895c8b2f8d1d61ae81d1a0.png)\n\nThis graph represents the interval $(-3,4).$ So, the union $(-3,2) \\cup (0,4)$ simplifies to the interval $(-3,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069950 #edb/ref 1065 1798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069950 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a4af4ad6-6c9c-552b-8ab9-8cb92744e7c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069950 #edb/ref 1067 "The Union of Overlapping Intervals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069998 #edb/ref 1064 "To simplify radical expressions, we can use the quotient rule for radicals, which states \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\sqrt {a}} {\\sqrt{b}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac a b}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}}$\n\nFirst, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}}  = \\dfrac {\\sqrt{16w^2}} {\\color{blue}\\sqrt{25}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5^2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression under the radical as a perfect square and taking its square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{16{w}^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(4w)^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4|\\cdot |w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\cdot |w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{4|w|}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069998 #edb/ref 1065 6691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069998 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7bfc07d9-feb0-5bcc-b3f0-50bb42eb2b6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069998 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069999 #edb/ref 1064 "The quotient rule for radicals also works for higher-order roots, like the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{ \\dfrac a b } = \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[3]{a} }{ \\sqrt[3]{b} }\n$$\n\nIn general, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{ \\dfrac a b } = \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[n]{a} }{ \\sqrt[n]{b} }\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069999 #edb/ref 1065 6692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069999 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a1d735da-05d5-5959-9226-229b7c29c497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069999 #edb/ref 1067 "The Quotient Rule for Cube Roots and Other Radicals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087686 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087686 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088072 #e